    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d576">

                <pb facs="0733" n="725" sameAs="frus:frus1938v02/pg725"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">741.65/660: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Italy</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Reed</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Rome</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-11-17T18:00:00+01:00">November 17, 1938—6
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received November 17—2:15 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>340. Commenting on the significance of the Italo-British accords Gayda<anchor
                        n="21" xml:id="d576-f1"/> this evening affirms that all the British
                    Dominions which <pb facs="0734" n="726" xml:id="pg726"/> have not yet done so
                    will now proceed to recognize the Empire, and then says “it will be of interest
                    to note at this point although without the slightest preoccupation that in
                    addition to these recognitions there are now lacking only those of Soviet Russia
                    and Roosevelt’s United States. This coincidence of notations and anti-historical
                    attitudes may well furnish food for useful thought in considering the general
                    picture of world policy.”</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Reed</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d576-f1" n="21" xml:id="d576-f1-content">
                <p>Virginio Gayda, spokesman for the Italian Foreign Office.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
